## Detailed Defects

### D1 — PROD `server_name` is still `knowtowin.com` (🔴 Critical)

**File**: `nginx.conf.template:43`

```nginx
server {
  listen 8080;
  server_name knowtowin.com;   ← must become app.knowtowin.com
```

This is the **primary task** of Workstream 1. The config has not been changed. Deploying the container as-is means the prod virtual host will answer for `knowtowin.com`, not `app.knowtowin.com`. Any request with `Host: app.knowtowin.com` would fall through to the DEV or localhost block, serving the wrong bundle.

**Fix**: Change `server_name knowtowin.com;` → `server_name app.knowtowin.com;`

During the transition window, the plan recommends temporarily including both:
```nginx
server_name app.knowtowin.com knowtowin.com;
```
Remove `knowtowin.com` once the apex is detached from this container.

---

### D2 — No global `X-Robots-Tag: noindex` on the PROD block (🔴 Critical)

**File**: `nginx.conf.template:41–133`

The DEV block (line 147) correctly has:
```nginx
add_header X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow" always;
```

The PROD block has **no such server-level directive**. The individual service-worker and utility locations have `X-Robots-Tag`, but any response served by the SPA fallback `location /`, `location = /index.html`, or the static-asset block carries **no `X-Robots-Tag`**.

The plan (Workstream 2) explicitly requires adding `X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow` to the production app host. This was not done. Google will freely crawl `app.knowtowin.com` as if it were indexable.

**Fix**: Add to the PROD `server {}` block near line 46:
```nginx
add_header X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow" always;
```

---

### D3 — `build/robots.txt` claims apex crawl ownership (🔴 Critical)

**File**: `build/robots.txt` (committed, served on what becomes `app.knowtowin.com`)

```
User-agent: *
Allow: /
Sitemap: https://knowtowin.com/sitemap.xml
```

This file:
1. Allows full crawl — contradicts the `noindex` intent for the app host
2. Points the Sitemap back to `knowtowin.com` — the app host is advertising the **apex sitemap** to crawlers

The plan (Workstream 2) says to override bundle crawl files or replace them. This has not been done. Until the bundle is regenerated or the Nginx config overrides this response, the app host will advertise itself as an open crawl surface with an apex-owned sitemap.

**Fix**: Either:
- Override in Nginx: `location = /robots.txt { return 200 "User-agent: *\nDisallow: /\n"; }`
- Or replace `build/robots.txt` with a disallow-all file as part of the normalization step

---

### D4 — `build/sitemap.xml` lists apex URLs (🔴 Critical)

**File**: `build/sitemap.xml` (committed, served on what becomes `app.knowtowin.com`)

```xml
<loc>https://knowtowin.com/</loc>
<loc>https://knowtowin.com/learn/</loc>
<loc>https://knowtowin.com/privacy</loc>
<loc>https://knowtowin.com/terms</loc>
<loc>https://knowtowin.com/email_help</loc>
```

When served from `app.knowtowin.com/sitemap.xml`, Google Search Console will associate this sitemap with the wrong property. The `loc` entries describe apex URLs, not app-subdomain URLs. This file should not be publicly served from the app host at all.

**Fix**: Override in Nginx to return 404 or empty: `location = /sitemap.xml { return 404; }`, or omit it entirely from `build/` during bundle normalization.

---

### D5 — Production bundle `index.html` preconnects to the dev API (🔴 Critical)

**File**: `build/index.html:43–44`

```html
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://dapi.knowtowin.com" crossorigin>
<link rel="dns-prefetch" href="//dapi.knowtowin.com">
```

`dapi.knowtowin.com` is the **dev/preprod API host**, not `api.knowtowin.com`. This is the bundle committed in `build/` (production) with `serviceWorkerVersion = "646966990"`. Every production page load will pre-connect to the dev API. If the dev API is not reachable from production context, this causes a wasted TLS handshake on every page load, and risks accidentally routing traffic to the wrong backend.

This is the same symptom as gap **G15** from the Flutter/backend analysis — the `settings.dart` `apiBaseUrl` is still toggled to the dev host, meaning the production bundle was built against the wrong endpoint.

**Fix**: Rebuild the production Flutter bundle with `--dart-define=API_BASE_URL=https://api.knowtowin.com`. The plan (§8) assumes this has been done before copying into `build/`.

---

### D6 — Font Awesome redirect creates a loop for `%2520`-encoded requests (🟠 High)

**File**: `nginx.conf.template:102–105` (PROD block)

```nginx
location ~* ^/assets/packages/font_awesome_flutter/lib/fonts/Font(?:%20|%2520)Awesome(?:%20|%2520)7(?:%20|%2520)(Brands-Regular-400|Free-Regular-400|Free-Solid-900)\.otf$ {
  add_header Cache-Control "private, no-store" always;
  return 302 /Font%20Awesome%207%20$1.otf?$canon_qs_prod;
}
```

The redirect target is `/Font%20Awesome%207%20$1.otf`. The top-level files in `build/` are:
- `Font Awesome 7 Brands-Regular-400.otf` (space-named — accessible as `Font%20Awesome%207%20Brands-Regular-400.otf`)
- `Font%20Awesome%207%20Brands-Regular-400.otf` (percent-named — accessible as `Font%2520Awesome%2520...` when double-encoded)

When a request comes for `Font%2520Awesome%2520...` (double-encoded, matched by `%2520`), Nginx redirects to `/Font%20Awesome%207%20$1.otf`. Nginx will then serve the literal file named `Font%20Awesome%207%20...otf` (the percent-encoded filename in the `build/` root), which is correct — this works.

However, the **static asset block** at line 108 also matches `.otf` files and enforces canonical versioning. A font request that hits the redirect, then follows the redirect URL, will be processed by the static asset block which checks `$need_vredir_prod`. If the `?version=` query string from the redirect is not exactly the canonical version, it will redirect again. Because `$canon_qs_prod` is appended to the redirect target, this should be stable on first redirect. **But** the redirect target `/Font%20Awesome%207%20$1.otf?$canon_qs_prod` appends the version from the map at template-render time — not from the actual request. Verify that `$canon_qs_prod` in the regex location context correctly expands via the http-level map. If not, the version redirect and font redirect interact to create multiple 302 hops per font request.

**Fix**: Add explicit `break` behavior or combine the Font Awesome redirect with the version query in a single step. At minimum this must be verified with browser network traces.

---

### D7 — `devbuild/` has no `robots.txt` (🟠 High)

**Observed**: `find` on `devbuild/` lists 28 files — no `robots.txt` present.

The DEV (`devapp.knowtowin.com`) server block has a global `X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow` header (line 147 — correct). However, the Nginx config **also explicitly serves `robots.txt` from the bundle**:

```nginx
# DEV block line 183:
location = /robots.txt { default_type text/plain; add_header Cache-Control "public, max-age=86400" always; try_files $uri =404; }
```

Since `devbuild/robots.txt` does not exist, this returns **404**. A crawler that checks `robots.txt` before crawling gets a 404, which by RFC means the crawler should assume **no restrictions** and crawl freely. Combined with the `X-Robots-Tag` header, the robots situation on devapp is inconsistent.

**Fix**: Either add an explicit `Disallow: /` robots.txt to `devbuild/`, or change the DEV robots location to return the disallow inline:
```nginx
location = /robots.txt { default_type text/plain; return 200 "User-agent: *\nDisallow: /\n"; }
```

---

### D8 — No `.well-known` location block anywhere in the config (🟠 High)

**File**: `nginx.conf.template` (entire file searched — zero results for `well-known`)

The plan (Workstream 6) says:
> "add a stable place in this repo for those files and ensure the Nginx config serves them without SPA fallback interference"

The SPA fallback at line 127–132 is:
```nginx
location / {
  try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;   ← SPA fallback
```

`/.well-known/assetlinks.json` must return the actual JSON file with the correct `Content-Type`, **not** the Flutter `index.html`. Without an explicit `location ^~ /.well-known/` block before the SPA fallback, Android and iOS app-link verification will either receive `index.html` (verification fails silently) or 404.

The plan correctly identifies this risk and recommends a fix — the config change has not been done.

**Fix**: Add to both PROD and DEV server blocks:
```nginx
location ^~ /.well-known/ {
  root /usr/share/nginx/html;   # or devhtml for DEV
  default_type application/json;
  add_header Cache-Control "public, max-age=3600" always;
  try_files $uri =404;
}
```
This must appear **before** the `location /` SPA fallback.

---

### D9 — Localhost block missing `Strict-Transport-Security` and `X-Robots-Tag` (🟡 Medium)

**File**: `nginx.conf.template:224–282`

The localhost block omits:
- `Strict-Transport-Security` (present in PROD and DEV blocks at lines 46/143)
- `X-Robots-Tag`

While localhost smoke checks happen over HTTP and HSTS is largely irrelevant there, the omission creates asymmetry that makes smoke-check results misleading (different response headers than prod). If this container is ever exposed via a tunnel (ngrok, local port-forward, etc.), the missing HSTS header is a real gap.

**Fix**: Add to the localhost block if symmetry with prod/dev is desired:
```nginx
add_header X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow" always;
```

---

### D10 — `envsubst` scope excludes potential `server_name` variables (🟡 Medium)

**File**: `docker-entrypoint.sh:92`

```sh
envsubst '$APP_VERSION_PROD $APP_VERSION_DEV' < /etc/nginx/nginx.conf.template > /etc/nginx/nginx.conf
```

`envsubst` is called with an **explicit variable allowlist** (`'$APP_VERSION_PROD $APP_VERSION_DEV'`). Only those two variables are substituted. If the `server_name` is later parameterized (e.g., `server_name $PROD_HOST;`) to make the host configurable via env vars, the entrypoint will silently leave the literal `$PROD_HOST` string in the rendered config, causing Nginx to start with a literal dollar-sign as the server name.

This is an operational footgun: the test (`nginx -g 'daemon off;'`) at the end may still pass because Nginx tolerates unknown server names, but requests will not route correctly.

**Fix**: Either:
- Document that `envsubst` scope must be expanded whenever a new `$VAR` is added to the template, or
- Remove the explicit allowlist to substitute all env vars (`envsubst < template > output`)

